<article class="env-definition" id="0GTI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GTI">Definition <span data-tag="0GTI">37.78.1</span>.</a> A morphism $f : X \to Y$ of schemes is said to be <em>completely decomposed</em><a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000864292" id="a0000864292-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> if for all points $y \in Y$ there is a point $x \in X$ with $f(x) = y$ such that the field extension $\kappa (x)/\kappa (y)$ is trivial. A family of morphisms $\{ f_ i : X_ i \to Y\} _{i \in I}$ of schemes with fixed target is said to be <em>completely decomposed</em> if $\coprod f_ i : \coprod Y_ i \to X$ is completely decomposed. </p>
</article>